---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Nav from '../../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
import NewsletterSignup from '../../components/NewsletterSignup.astro';
import { reviewedByJabin } from '../../lib/person.js';
import { techArticleSchema } from '../../lib/techarticle.js';

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'Does this cheat sheet do the math for me?',
    a: 'No — it\'s a quick reference for which formula applies and what it needs, not a calculator. Each one links to the real, interactive calculator on this site where you actually enter your numbers and get a result.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why not just list worked examples here?',
    a: 'Because a worked example uses someone else\'s numbers, not yours. The calculators are free, show their formulas openly, and take seconds to run with your actual figures — that\'s a safer habit to build than memorizing a sample answer.',
  },
];

// One primary entity for this URL: a TechArticle. It is a reference sheet,
// not an app, so the WebApplication block it used to carry alongside (two
// competing primary entities for one URL) is gone. reviewedBy is a WebPage
// property in schema.org, so it sits on the page's own WebPage node, which
// the TechArticle names as mainEntityOfPage. Dates come from git via the same
// map as the sitemap lastmod (src/lib/techarticle.js).
const jsonLd = techArticleSchema({
  sourcePath: 'src/pages/calculators/formulas-cheat-sheet.astro',
  path: '/calculators/formulas-cheat-sheet',
  headline: "Drilling Formulas Quick-Reference Cheat Sheet",
  description: "Core drilling formulas, conversions, and a worked example for the rig floor: hydrostatic pressure, kill mud weight, MAASP, ECD, buoyancy, capacity.",
  lang: 'en',
  extra: {
    about: { "@type": "Thing", "name": "Drilling and well control formulas" },
    mainEntityOfPage: { "@id": "https://rigfloorhq.com/calculators/formulas-cheat-sheet#webpage" },
  },
});

const webPageLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "WebPage",
  "@id": "https://rigfloorhq.com/calculators/formulas-cheat-sheet#webpage",
  "url": "https://rigfloorhq.com/calculators/formulas-cheat-sheet",
  "name": "Drilling Formulas Cheat Sheet — Quick Reference",
  "inLanguage": "en",
  "isPartOf": { "@id": "https://rigfloorhq.com/#website" },
  "mainEntity": { "@id": "https://rigfloorhq.com/calculators/formulas-cheat-sheet#article" },
  "reviewedBy": reviewedByJabin(),
  "lastReviewed": "2026-10-17"
};
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Drilling Formulas Cheat Sheet — Quick Reference"
  description="Core drilling formulas, conversions, and a worked example for the rig floor: hydrostatic pressure, kill mud weight, MAASP, ECD, buoyancy, capacity."
  breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Calculators', href: '/calculators' }, { label: 'Formulas Cheat Sheet', href: '/calculators/formulas-cheat-sheet' }]}
  lang="en"
  alternateHref="/es/calculators/formulas-cheat-sheet"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
  extraJsonLd={[webPageLd]}
>
  <Nav slot="nav" lang="en" alternateHref="/es/calculators/formulas-cheat-sheet" />
  <Footer slot="footer" lang="en" />

  <div class="page-hero">
    <div class="page-hero-eyebrow">Quick Reference</div>
    <h1>Drilling Formulas <span>Cheat Sheet</span></h1>
    <p class="page-hero-sub">The eight formulas that come up most on the rig floor — well control and basic string math — in one place, plus the unit conversions, the constants they're built from, and a full worked example end to end. This is a reference for which formula applies and what it needs — for the actual number, use the linked calculator with your real figures.</p>
    <p style="font-size:13px;color:var(--fog);margin-top:8px;">Reviewed for accuracy by <a href="/about/jabin-travis" style="color:var(--amber);">Jabin Travis</a>, IADC WellSharp-certified driller.</p>
  </div>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="prose">
      <p>Every formula below is reviewed for accuracy by <a href="/about/jabin-travis" style="color:var(--amber);">Jabin Travis</a>, IADC WellSharp-certified driller, same as the calculators themselves.</p>
    </div>

    <dl class="formula-block">
      <dt class="formula-label">Hydrostatic Pressure</dt>
      <dd class="formula-eq"><code>HP (psi) = 0.052 × MW (ppg) × TVD (ft)</code></dd>
      <dd class="formula-note">The static mud column pressure at a given depth. Run it: <a href="/calculators/hydrostatic">Hydrostatic Pressure Calculator</a>.</dd>
    </dl>

    <dl class="formula-block">
      <dt class="formula-label">Kill Mud Weight</dt>
      <dd class="formula-eq"><code>KMW = OMW + (SIDPP ÷ (0.052 × TVD))</code></dd>
      <dd class="formula-note">The mud weight needed to balance the well after a kick, from shut-in drill pipe pressure. Run it: <a href="/calculators/kill-sheet">Kill Sheet Calculator</a>.</dd>
    </dl>

    <dl class="formula-block">
      <dt class="formula-label">Maximum Allowable Annular Surface Pressure</dt>
      <dd class="formula-eq"><code>MAASP = LOT/FIT Pressure + [(LOT/FIT MW − Current MW) × 0.052 × Shoe TVD]</code></dd>
      <dd class="formula-note">The ceiling on surface pressure before the shoe takes fluid. Run it: <a href="/calculators/maasp">MAASP Calculator</a>.</dd>
    </dl>

    <dl class="formula-block">
      <dt class="formula-label">Kick Tolerance</dt>
      <dd class="formula-eq"><code>KT = [MAASP − (Influx Height × MW × 0.052)] ÷ (0.052 × Shoe TVD)</code></dd>
      <dd class="formula-note">How much kick the well can take before MAASP is exceeded. Run it: <a href="/calculators/kick-tolerance">Kick Tolerance Calculator</a>.</dd>
    </dl>

    <dl class="formula-block">
      <dt class="formula-label">Equivalent Circulating Density</dt>
      <dd class="formula-eq"><code>ECD = MW + (Annular Friction Loss ÷ 0.052 ÷ TVD)</code></dd>
      <dd class="formula-note">The effective mud weight while circulating, once friction pressure is added. Run it: <a href="/calculators/ecd">ECD Calculator</a>.</dd>
    </dl>

    <dl class="formula-block">
      <dt class="formula-label">Trip Margin / Safety Factor</dt>
      <dd class="formula-eq"><code>TM/SF = Base Mud Weight + [Safety Margin ÷ (0.052 × TVD)]</code></dd>
      <dd class="formula-note">Extra mud weight carried to cover swab pressure while tripping. Run it: <a href="/calculators/trip-margin">Trip Margin Calculator</a>.</dd>
    </dl>

    <h2 id="buoyancy" class="mt-24">Buoyancy &amp; String Weight</h2>

    <dl class="formula-block">
      <dt class="formula-label">Buoyancy Factor</dt>
      <dd class="formula-eq"><code>BF = 1 − (Fluid Weight (ppg) ÷ 65.5)</code></dd>
      <dd class="formula-note">The fraction of a string's air weight it actually hangs as once submerged in fluid. Run it: <a href="/calculators/buoyancy">Buoyancy &amp; Tubular Weight Calculator</a>.</dd>
    </dl>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>A note on 65.5: steel at 489.5 lb/ft³ works out at <strong>65.44 ppg</strong>, not 65.5. The rounded figure is the field convention and the difference is under a tenth of a percent, but if a number will not reconcile against a supplier's tally book, this is one of the places the discrepancy hides.</p>
    </div>

    <dl class="formula-block">
      <dt class="formula-label">Neutral Point</dt>
      <dd class="formula-eq"><code>Max WOB (lb) = Collar weight (lb/ft) × Length (ft) × BF</code></dd>
      <dd class="formula-note">Keep the neutral point in the collars. Putting drill pipe in compression is how you buy a fatigue failure. More on pipe grades and inspection class: <a href="/equipment/drill-string">drill string page</a>.</dd>
    </dl>

    <dl class="formula-block">
      <dt class="formula-label">Capacity &amp; Displacement</dt>
      <dd class="formula-eq"><code>Capacity (bbl/ft) = ID² ÷ 1029.4<br />Displacement (bbl/ft) = (OD² − ID²) ÷ 1029.4</code></dd>
      <dd class="formula-note">Volume per foot inside the pipe, and volume per foot the pipe itself displaces. Run it: <a href="/calculators/drill-pipe-tally">Drill Pipe Tally Calculator</a>.</dd>
    </dl>

    <h2 id="conversions" class="mt-24">Mud Weight Conversions</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>The same fluid gets reported four different ways depending on who is writing the report. These are exact, not rounded.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>From ppg, multiply by</th><th>To get</th><th>12.0 ppg becomes</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>÷ 8.3454</td><td>Specific gravity</td><td>1.438</td></tr>
          <tr><td>÷ 19.25</td><td>psi/ft</td><td>0.6234</td></tr>
          <tr><td>× 7.4805</td><td>lb/ft³</td><td>89.77</td></tr>
          <tr><td>× 119.826</td><td>kg/m³</td><td>1,437.9</td></tr>
          <tr><td>÷ 8.5099</td><td>bar/10 m</td><td>1.410</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>Note that <strong>19.25 is the exact reciprocal of the 0.052 constant</strong> — they are the same conversion written two ways, which is worth knowing when a figure looks off by a factor you cannot place. The <a href="/calculators/mud-weight-converter">mud weight converter</a> does all five live.</p>
    </div>

    <h2 id="connections" class="mt-24">Connections: Flange Ratings &amp; Make-Up Torque</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>Not every number on the rig floor is a formula — some are lookups, and getting them wrong matters just as much. A BOP stack's flanged connections carry an API 6A/6BX rating tied to the stack's own pressure class; the <a href="/calculators/flange-ring-gasket">flange and ring gasket lookup</a> goes from bore size and pressure rating to the correct ring gasket, stud count, and stud size.</p>
      <p>A bit's connection to the string is a different system entirely — a tapered thread made up to a specific torque rather than a gasketed bolt-up. The <a href="/calculators/bit-torque">bit thread and make-up torque lookup</a> goes from bit size to thread connection, then to the Minimum, Maximum, and Maximum Severe torque for PDC and roller cone bits.</p>
    </div>

    <h2 id="survey" class="mt-24">Directional &amp; Survey</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>Every depth on this page assumes the drill pipe tally and the rig floor reference it is measured from have not moved. When a rig floor elevation actually changes — a different substructure, a workover rig on a re-entry — reconcile it with the <a href="/calculators/odf-depth-correction">rig floor (ODF) depth correction calculator</a> before trusting a tally against it.</p>
    </div>

    <dl class="formula-block">
      <dt class="formula-label">TVD over a Constant-Inclination Section</dt>
      <dd class="formula-eq"><code>ΔTVD (ft) = ΔMD (ft) × cos I</code></dd>
      <dd class="formula-note">I = inclination from vertical. In a build section use minimum curvature rather than this.</dd>
    </dl>

    <dl class="formula-block">
      <dt class="formula-label">Build Rate</dt>
      <dd class="formula-eq"><code>Build rate (°/100 ft) = (I₂ − I₁) × 100 ÷ Course length (ft)</code></dd>
      <dd class="formula-note">Inclination change only. It is not the same as dogleg severity unless azimuth is constant.</dd>
    </dl>

    <dl class="formula-block">
      <dt class="formula-label">Dogleg Severity</dt>
      <dd class="formula-eq"><code>cos β = cos(I₂ − I₁) − sin I₁ × sin I₂ × (1 − cos(A₂ − A₁))<br />DLS (°/100 ft) = β × 100 ÷ Course length (ft)</code></dd>
      <dd class="formula-note">The minimum curvature dogleg angle β, accounting for both inclination and azimuth change. When azimuth is unchanged it collapses to the build rate above.</dd>
    </dl>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>Well profiles, build sections and why dogleg severity limits matter are on the <a href="/drilling/directional">directional drilling page</a>.</p>
    </div>

    <h2 id="constants" class="mt-24">The Constants, and Where They Come From</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>Every rounded constant on this page is a unit conversion, not a fudge factor. Knowing the derivation is what lets you rebuild one when a figure looks wrong.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Constant</th><th>Exact value</th><th>What it is</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>0.052</td><td>0.0519481</td><td>12 ÷ 231 — cubic inches per foot of a 1 in² column, over cubic inches per gallon</td></tr>
          <tr><td>19.25</td><td>19.25</td><td>Exactly 1 ÷ 0.0519481. The same conversion inverted</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1029.4</td><td>1029.4142</td><td>Barrel volume ÷ area constant, giving bbl per foot from inches squared</td></tr>
          <tr><td>24.5</td><td>24.5099</td><td>gpm to ft/min through an annulus measured in inches</td></tr>
          <tr><td>0.000243</td><td>0.00024286</td><td>Three cylinders, per stroke, expressed in barrels</td></tr>
          <tr><td>8.3454</td><td>8.345404</td><td>Pounds per gallon of fresh water — the specific gravity anchor</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1 bbl</td><td>42 gal = 5.6146 ft³</td><td>9,702 cubic inches</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2 id="worked" class="mt-24">One Worked Example, End to End</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>A 12.0 ppg mud at 10,000 ft TVD. The well kicks and is shut in on 400 psi drillpipe, 550 psi casing, with a 12 bbl pit gain in a 8½ in hole around 5 in pipe.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Hydrostatic:</strong> 0.052 × 12.0 × 10,000 = <strong>6,240 psi</strong></li>
        <li><strong>Formation pressure:</strong> 6,240 + 400 = <strong>6,640 psi</strong></li>
        <li><strong>Kill mud weight:</strong> 12.0 + 400 ÷ (0.052 × 10,000) = 12.0 + 0.77 = <strong>12.77 ppg</strong>, call it 12.8</li>
        <li><strong>Annular capacity:</strong> (8.5² − 5²) ÷ 1029.4 = (72.25 − 25) ÷ 1029.4 = <strong>0.0459 bbl/ft</strong></li>
        <li><strong>Influx height:</strong> 12 ÷ 0.0459 = <strong>261 ft</strong></li>
        <li><strong>Influx density:</strong> 12.0 − (550 − 400) ÷ (0.052 × 261) = 12.0 − 11.05 = <strong>0.95 ppg</strong> — gas</li>
      </ul>
      <p>That last line is the one that changes the shift. A 0.95 ppg influx is gas, gas migrates, and a gas kick shut in on a well that is not being circulated will carry its pressure to surface whether or not anyone is watching the gauges. The arithmetic took a minute; the conclusion decides what happens for the next twelve hours.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="note-box">
      <strong>These are the standard field equations, not a substitute for your well programme.</strong> Rig-specific procedures, your operator's well control policy and the standard governing your jurisdiction take precedence over anything on this page. Check pump output by measurement, check slow circulating rates on tour, and verify fracture gradient against the actual leak-off test rather than a gradient assumption.
    </div>

    <div class="prose mt-24">
      <p>For the full concept behind these — how pore pressure, fracture pressure, and mud weight window all tie together — see the <a href="/pillars/hydrostatic-pressure">hydrostatic pressure reference page</a>. For the complete list of tools, back to <a href="/calculators">all drilling calculators</a>. Knowing these formulas cold, not just looking them up, is what separates a <a href="/careers/driller">driller</a> from the crew below — pay and the full driller job picture are in the <a href="/careers/driller">career guide</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <NewsletterSignup lang="en" />
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="section-eyebrow">FAQ</div>
    <h2 class="section-title">Frequently Asked <span>Questions</span></h2>
    <div class="accordion">
      {faq.map((item) => (
        <details class="acc-item">
          <summary class="acc-header">{item.q}<span class="acc-arrow">▼</span></summary>
          <div class="acc-body"><p set:html={item.a} /></div>
        </details>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

